  {
    "@id": "http://purl.org/spar/po",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology"
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/contributor": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "Sebastian Barzaghi (https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0799-1527)"
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/creator": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "Angelo Di Iorio (https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6893-7452)"
      },
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "Fabio Vitali (https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7562-5203)"
      },
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "Silvio Peroni (https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0530-4305)"
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/date": [
      {
        "@value": "2026-10-05"
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "\nThe **_Pattern Ontology (PO)_** is an OWL 2 DL ontology that formally defines patterns for segmenting a document into atomic components, in order to be manipulated independently and re-flowed in different contexts.\n\n![The main architecture of PO.](po.png)\n\nStructural patterns allow representing document elements (such as sections, paragraphs, lists, metadata, and tables) through abstract, reusable primitives independent of specific markup syntaxes.\n\n## Examples of use\n\nIn the following subsections, we introduce some examples to showcase how to use PO. \n\nThe prefixes that are used in all the examples provided below are defined as follows:\n\n        @prefix : <http://sparontologies.net/example/> .\n        @prefix po: <http://www.essepuntato.it/2008/12/pattern#> .\n        @prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .\n\n### Structural decomposition of a document section\n\nWith PO, it is possible to represent document elements and how they manifest as patterns occurring within the document's structure itself.\n\nFor example, the following example shows how to use PO to model a section defined as a headed container and containing a heading block as its header and a body paragraph block containing inline text and atom elements.\n        \n        :section-1 a po:HeadedContainer ;\n                po:hasName \"section\" ;\n                po:containsAsHeader :heading-1 ;\n                po:contains :paragraph-1 .\n\n        :heading-1 a po:Block ;\n                po:hasName \"h1\" ;\n                po:contains :text-heading .\n\n        :paragraph-1 a po:Block ;\n                po:hasName \"p\" ;\n                po:contains :text-span .\n\n        :text-span a po:Inline ;\n                po:hasName \"span\" ;\n                po:contains :atom-text .\n\n        :text-heading a po:Atom ;\n                po:hasName \"text\" .\n\n        :atom-text a po:Atom ;\n                po:hasName \"text\" .\n\n## Competency Questions\n\nPO can be used for answering several questions related to the atomic components that form a document according to certain patterns.\nIn the following subsections, some of them are introduced together with their respective SPARQL queries. \n\nThe prefixes that are used in all the SPARQL queries provided below are defined as follows:\n\n        PREFIX po: <http://www.essepuntato.it/2008/12/pattern#>\n\n### CQ1\n\nWhich header elements are contained within a headed container?\n        \n        SELECT ?header WHERE {\n                ?section a po:headedContainer ;\n                        po:containsAsHeader ?header .\n        }\n\n### CQ2\n\nWhich block elements are directly contained within a container?\n\n        SELECT ?block WHERE {\n                ?section po:contains ?block .\n                ?block a po:Block .\n        }\n\n"
      }
    ],
